  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0004] Exclude bool when validating integers: When validating an integer argument, check isinstance(x, bool) first because bool is a subclass of int; otherwise True/False pass as quantities.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary results with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 1x, 1 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0002] Use to_money and ZERO from ledgerly.money: In ledgerly/invoice.py, coerce unit prices with to_money() and seed sums with ZERO from ledgerly.money, mirroring line_total and invoice_total.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 1x, 1 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with stable code strings: Reject invalid billing input by raising BillingError(code, detail) from ledgerly.errors, reusing existing codes like invalid_state and negative_amount rather than inventing new exception types.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)",
